{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-41566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Write $3-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}$ as a single fraction." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-41566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a-3$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{a-3}{a-3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we subtract the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(a-3)}{a-3}-\\frac{a+2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(a-3)-(a+2)}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a-9-a-2}{a-3} & = \\\\ \\frac{2a-11}{a-3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-41566" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-41566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-41566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{2a-11}{a-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{2a-7}{a-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4a+11}{a-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4a-11}{a-3}"] ["math" "\\frac{4a-7}{a-3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-41566" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2a-11}{a-3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-7024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $f(x)=-\\frac{6}{11}x+3$, then ${f}^{-1}(x)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-7024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{6}{11}x+3\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=-\\frac{6}{11}y+3\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-\\frac{6}{11}y+3 \\\\ x-3 & =-\\frac{6}{11}y \\\\ 11(x-3) & =-6y \\\\ 11x-33 & =-6y \\\\ -\\frac{11x-33}{6} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\frac{11x-33}{6}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-7024" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-7024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-7024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-\\frac{11x-33}{6}"] ["math" "-\\frac{11}{6}x+3"] ["math" "-\\frac{2x-4}{11}"] ["math" "\\frac{11}{6}x-3"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{11x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-7024" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{11x-33}{6}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-103440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(2,3)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-103440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope ${m}_{1}$ of the line passing through the points $(1,4)$ and $(-4,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{-4-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5})=-1\\,⇒\\,m=\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(2,3).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}(x-2) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{5}{2}x-5 \\\\ y & =\\frac{5}{2}x-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-103440" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-103440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-103440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=-\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{19}{5}"] ["math" "y=-\\frac{5}{2}x+8"] ["math" "y=\\frac{1}{2}x+2"] ["math" "y=\\frac{2}{5}x+\\frac{11}{5}"] ["math" "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-103440" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=\\frac{5}{2}x-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-263419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the variance of the following data set?\n\n$$\n3,\\,18,\\,16,\\,7,\\,1,\\,12,\\,5,\\,10\n$$\n\nDo not round your answer.\n\n${\\sigma }^{2}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-263419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If we have a data set that consists of the points ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$ then the variance can be computed as\n\n$$\n{\\sigma }^{2}=\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overset{―}{x}$ is the mean of the data set and $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}$ is the mean of the squares of the points in the data set.\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=8$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (3+18+16+7+1+12+5+10) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 72 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot ({3}^{2}+{18}^{2}+{16}^{2}+{7}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{12}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{10}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot (9+324+256+49+1+144+25+100) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 908 \\\\  & =\\frac{908}{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{227}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{227}{2}-{(9)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{227}{2}-81 \\\\  & =\\frac{227-162}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{65}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-263419" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-263419" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-263419" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "32.5"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-72996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the functions $e(x)$ and $n(x),$ you're given the following data:\n\n$$\ne(-5)=5,\\,{e}^{′}(-5)=-6,\\,n(-5)=-7,\\,{n}^{′}(-5)=9\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))$ at $x=-5.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-72996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Using the sum and constant multiple rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))=3{e}^{′}(x)+{n}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=-5$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3e(x)+n(x))|}_{x=-5} & =3{e}^{′}(-5)+{n}^{′}(-5) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-6)+9 \\\\  & =-18+9 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-72996" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-72996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-72996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-18"] ["math" "-27"] ["math" "-9"] ["math" "18"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-72996" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-9"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-71873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y={e}^{2{x}^{4}},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-71873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u=2{x}^{4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (8{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{u} \\\\  & =8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-71873" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-71873" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-71873" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "4{x}^{2}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}"] ["math" "8x{e}^{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}"] ["math" "8{x}^{3}{e}^{{x}^{3}}"] ["math" "9{x}^{2}{e}^{8{x}^{4}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-71873" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8{x}^{3}{e}^{2{x}^{4}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-24072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "If $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}<\\theta <\\pi $ then what is the value of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-24072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's sketch our angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/assets/b81b4602d221eb04aab76b0ed8853ab75a5c3cf93e76cc489344bc15133c4daf.png)\n\nWe're given that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}.$ We can use the Pythagorean identity to find $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{5}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2}{5})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{4}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{21}{25}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{2}{5})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{21}}{5})}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{21}}=-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-24072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-24072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-24072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{63}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{63}"] ["math" "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3\\sqrt{21}}{105}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-24072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{21}}{21}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-29838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-29838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we apply the constant factor rule and take the coefficient $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ out of the integral. Then we compute the integral of $\\frac{1}{x},$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{x\\sqrt{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\int \\frac{1}{x}\\,\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-29838" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-29838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-29838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(|x|+C)"] ["math" "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}|x|}{2}+C)"] ["math" "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}|x|)+C"] ["math" "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-29838" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x|+C"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the range of the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2-6x}-2\\,?$\n\nThe range is\n$f(x)\\in (${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$)\\cup (${{field-3}}$,\\infty ).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A transformed reciprocal function of the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{a}{bx+c}+d\n$$\n\nhas the horizontal asymptote $y=d,$ and therefore, its range is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,d)\\cup (d,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $d=-2.$ Therefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=-2,$ and the range of the function is\n\n$$\nf(x)\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (-2,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe graph $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/assets/27f800d93b5d4c84e98d0b7332388c5b3d513e4b896adabe6e410e4c3e3b684c.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ac85066f8c2132e5a3ef77be90d68439e8f489c8723e2ecaca34a3395977c387"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\infty"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13993337/state.json" "file_sha256" "af44452919480e68c40f1d4ad31a12db3182781ed4920bf96dec04bec1947dab"} {"question" "q-276683" "field" "field-3" 